A terminal shared across clients.

A terminal belongs to the server, not to the browser tab that opened it. Workspace
identity is canonicalised from the folder path, so the same workspace reached
through a different origin (localhost against a LAN address) is one workspace,
and it is the only identity a backend reports - the one it answers to as well as
the one it sends. Layouts merge across clients instead of the last writer
winning; attach state is per client and only the final detach releases the pty.
Replays are bound to the renderer that asked for them, so concurrent starts do
not cross wires, and live data reaches a client only once its own replay has
landed. Single-recipient events go to the one client that can act on them: the
UI target for a terminal, the first client of a workspace for a reply the pty
host accepts once, and every client of the workspace for a detach request, which
names an instance only its own window holds.

Each of those rules is an overlay module this patch calls, so it can be run by a
test rather than only read off a diff: terminalWorkspaceId.ts is the identity,
terminalLayoutMerge.ts the merge, terminalClientState.ts who holds a terminal and
which of them a single-recipient event goes to, terminalStartState.ts which
client a replay belongs to.

Which client sizes the pty, and which clients' acknowledgements advance its flow
control, are not here - the pty host arbitrates both per client, through a
registry of its own (terminal-clients.diff).

